   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs in collaboration with the social partners, drafted and revised the national child protection strategy between November 2020 and June 2021. Validation with social partners is completed and officially adopted in 2021.
Revisions to the strategy covered prohibition of the use of children in illegal activities in line with ILO Convention No.182, such as performing hazardous work which  interferes with the child&apos;s education and is harmful to the child&apos;s development. It also included a clause indicating that employment of children between 15-18 years of age should respect the articles of chapter 11 in the Iraqi labour law. 
In line with Convention 138 on the minimum age, the government is cooperating with representative organisations of workers and employers to finalize a strategy related to child protection and labour. 
This strategy which is gender-responsive incorporates the basic principles that there shall be no discrimination on the grounds of the gender of the child nor of the parent or legal guardian. 
The national tripartite steering committee to tackle the Worst Forms of Child Labour was established in March 2021. The objectives of this steering committee is to support the project&apos;s national ownership, provide policy guidance, prioritization, and promote its overall objectives within Government, Parliament, agencies and institutions, members of employers&apos; and workers&apos; organisations, and other stakeholders in order to create greater awareness, understanding and visibility and to ensure the project&apos;s impact is as wide-ranging as possible.
An awareness raising campaign was launched to tackle the Worst Forms of Child Labour in Iraq in partnership with the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs, UN agencies, CSOs and social partners during a virtual event in May 2021. With the support of the European Union, the Czech Republic, Denmark, Ireland and Switzerland through the Regional Development and Protection Programme (RDPP), the ILO built the capacity of the government and the constituents through workshops organized in August 2020. The ILO supported the drafting of the policy in December 2020. The ILO co-organized a workshop in June 2021 to help the social partners ensure the right of children in accordance with international labour standards, taking into consideration relevant international standards, in particular recommendations of convention 182 on the Worst Forms of Child Labour and convention 138 to allow children to grow, develop and live with freedom and dignity. This workshop resulted in the validation of the strategy by social partners.
The validation was conducted in cooperation with UNICEF throughout the workshop and validation sessions, beside the government, UN agencies, CSO, and social partners were present for validation and provide input.  
ILO was instrumental in the establishment of a national steering committee, to tackle the Worst Forms of Child Labour through providing ToR, guidance, and suggestion of membership and working mechanism. 
The ILO facilitated the launch of  a national campaign to raise awareness to tackle the Worst Forms of Child Labour in Iraq in partnership with the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs, UN agencies, CSOs and social partners. The target groups include children, their parents/guardians, government, and constituents at different levels, as well as building capacity of local media.The campaign aims to raise awareness on the increasing danger of child labour, inform communities about the negative consequences of child labour and ultimately change behaviour of society and institutions alike. It also aims to make child labour unacceptable as an urgent priority for responsible authorities and worker and employer representatives to tackle and reject the Worst forms of Child Labour.</narrative>
   </description>
